# Lean & Python Ledger
> The Lorentz Factor as the Slope of an Additional Spatial Coordinate: Embedding, Painlevé–Gullstrand Flow, and the Issue Of Simultaneity

<Admonition title="What is this?" warn foldable sidebar right>
This entire note, apart from this admonition and a few syntax tweaks to render nicely in a Conundrum file, has been generated with the help of AI by **[Pablo Nogueira Grossi](https://github.com/TOTOGT/DM3-lab)**. Obviously some things like "the author hasn't seen it" have been left in to remove personal influence on the model's output.

The associated Lean file can be downloaded **[here](/assets/download/lean_python/check_lean.lean)**, the python (numeric) file **[here](/assets/download/lean_python/check_numbers.py)**, and the symbolic python file **[here](/assets/download/lean_python/check_sympy.py)**. All of these are checks of the model and are not the same code that was used during the development of the theory; you can find some of the code that was part of my personal workflow during the development of this theory, published as a Python package **[here](https://pypi.org/project/alpha-omega-gravity/0.1.3/)**.
</Admonition>

**[Zenodo preprint](https://zenodo.org/records/24280888)**, v1, 16 November 2026 (A. Mueller, independent researcher). Prepared 20 Nov 2026 (G6 LLC). Working ledger, unreviewed by a specialist or a second system. The author has not seen it. Nothing in this folder has been sent to the author or to anyone.

## Scope: what was read and what was not

Read in full or in the part that carries claims: abstract; §2.2.1–2.2.2 (Lorentz factor as a slope); §2.3.2–2.3.5 ($G_{44}$, Cassini, vacuum equation for $G_{44}$); §2.4.3–2.4.6 (induced geometry, curvature, temporal closure); §2.5 and §2.5.1 (Proposition 1, interior solution, Buchdahl); §2.6–2.8.4 (orbits as summarised, higher-dimensional metric, Painlevé–Gullstrand, Hubble flow); §3 (Hypothesis M and the Milky Way comparison); §4.6–4.7; Appendix A introduction, A.4, A.5, Table A3.

Not read: §1 and §1.1 (introduction and the lighthouse text, beyond the blog version), §2.1 (dictionary) and Table 1; §2.3.6 (matter-sourced equation for the fifth-dimensional metric) and §2.4.1–2.4.2 (off-diagonal terms); §4.1–4.5; Appendix A.1–A.3 and A.6 (fine-structure and electromagnetic alignments); the derivations in Appendix B (only the results quoted in §2.6 were checked); the reference list. The rotation-curve data (Eilers et al.) and the baryonic profiles (Cautun et al., McMillan) were not available, so no $\chi^2$ was recomputed.

The author's own scope statements are taken as stated: the Hamiltonian constraint and the static vacuum equations are imported from general relativity (§2.4.4, §2.5, §2.8.4); the construction is "equivalent to general relativity" only in vacuum or pressureless matter with spherical symmetry; Hypothesis M is an assumption with a one-galaxy comparison that "does not determine $\kappa'$".

## Files

- `MuellerChecks.lean`: Lean 4 + Mathlib, 24 theorems, no `sorry`, axiom reports for the nine headline theorems show only propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound.
- `check_sympy.py`: symbolic checks that need second derivatives of square roots (ALL PASS).
- `check_numbers.py`: quoted numbers recomputed from public constants, with controls (ALL PASS).

